# Line endings
#
# POSIX shells do not treat CR as whitespace: in a CRLF checkout the word `fi\r`
# is not the `fi` keyword, so an `if` block never closes and the parser dies at
# the next construct it cannot absorb — "syntax error near unexpected token
# `$'{\r'`". Two paths in this repo make that a real failure rather than a
# cosmetic one, and both bite only when the build host is Windows:
#
#   scripts/*.sh                    run inside the Docker build container, so a
#                                   CRLF checkout breaks `dev.sh build`
#
#   internal/shellinit/scripts/**   go:embed'ed INTO the daemon binary. A
#                                   Windows-built quild therefore ships CRLF
#                                   inside itself and breaks shell integration
#                                   on every Linux host it runs on — including
#                                   a remote daemon reached via `quil --remote`,
#                                   where the symptom is a broken prompt AND a
#                                   PATH missing everything ~/.bashrc adds,
#                                   because the failed parse aborts before the
#                                   script sources it.
#
# Marking them eol=lf keeps the working tree LF on every platform, so what
# go:embed sees is what git stores.
*.sh text eol=lf
internal/shellinit/scripts/** text eol=lf

# Changelog fragments are concatenated verbatim into CHANGELOG.md by
# scripts/promote-changelog.sh. A fragment authored on Windows would otherwise
# carry CRLF into a file that is LF everywhere else — the goreleaser job strips
# \r from the release NOTES (release.yml), so the damage would be invisible on
# the release page and permanent in the committed changelog. The promoter also
# strips CR defensively; this keeps it from ever being needed.
changelog.d/*.md text eol=lf
# CHANGELOG.md is what the promoter WRITES, splicing fragment bytes into the
# existing file. Its blob is already LF, but without this the working tree is
# CRLF under core.autocrlf, so a local promote produces a file that is LF on
# the lines it just wrote and CRLF on the history it copied through.
CHANGELOG.md text eol=lf

# internal/changelog/highlights.txt is go:embed'ed and parsed line by line, so a
# CRLF checkout puts a trailing \r at the end of every record — which lands
# inside the headline text and is rendered into the TUI. Same failure class as
# internal/shellinit/scripts/** above; internal/remoteinstall/embed_test.go is
# the precedent for pairing the attribute with a no-CR assertion.
internal/changelog/highlights.txt text eol=lf
